  "text": "When it comes to the Substack experience, I’ve been through it all.\n\nI published my first article on December 26, 2022 and wrote every week for 6 months. I got 60 subscribers. \n\nRoughly half of all Substack publications go quiet after 6 months, and I was no exception.\n\nI didn’t publish again for one and a half years.\n\n———\n\nI came back at the beginning of 2025 and published articles once a week to once a month for 10 months. I barely wrote any Notes. I grew from 60 to 100 subscribers.\n\nOn October 31, I decided — I wanted to be more active in the Substack community. \n\nSo I started writing Notes more frequently, making restacks, and writing replies and comments. It took me awhile to get used to being myself on a public digital platform, and I was delightfully surprised by how much the conversations we have on here are genuine, deep, and fun.\n\nSpecial thank yous to  @Jason Ives @Natalie Nicholson @AI Meets Girlboss @Dallas▫️ @Caitlin McColl 🇨🇦 @Mia Kiraki 🎭 @Jen Benford @Dinah @Code Like A Girl @SheWritesAI @Kim Doyal @Rebecca Spitzerfor being early supporters and friends. I wouldn’t be here without you.\n\nAnd a special special shoutout to @Dr Sam Illingworthwho encouraged me to keep writing long replies and Notes when I had a lot of doubt that I should. But when a Professor of Critical AI Literacy and poet / playwright / author tells you to lean into your writing style, you do it!\n\n———\n\nIn the last 9 months, I’ve grown from 100 to 970 unique subscribers across three newsletters (Step Up Step Together has 842; the HART Studio has 210; Create with Alyssa has 51.)\n\n———\n\nIt is surreal that I could get 1000 subscribers. \n\nTo me, folks in this range and beyond feel like celebrities, people who have something special about them that I don’t have. I feel so ordinary, how did this happen!?\n\nI share these stats and this story because it shocks me that I could get here by just being me, showing up consistently, and connecting with wonderful, amazing people like you.\n\nAnd I want you to know, you can too. \n\nThis journey can feel slow. That’s because building genuine connections that matter take time. Give them a chance to simmer. They’ll grow when they’re ready. ❤️",
