  "text": "I’m exhausted, and my heart is full.\n\nWhat a bonkers week, amiright?\n\nI wrote a couple Notes about the Substack-Pangram thing, and they blew up. I don’t know that I’ve ever had Notes with this much engagement, let alone two in a row.\n\nI was nervous to say anything. My biggest fear was getting jumped on by the anti-AI crowd, and I don’t do well with people who just want to tear others down.\n\nBut I had to say something. \n\nI kept picturing a person, a woman, someone who has been trying to share their voice and doing it with AI. I saw them shrinking into the shadows because of the unwanted spotlight by this feature. \n\nAnd it’s just not right. \n\nPeople who are curious, open, willing to experiment, willing to fail! (because anyone who works with AI knows it’s not about “learning how to use it.” It’s about learning where it doesn’t work and how to adapt) should be allowed to do that in a safe environment, not one where they have to watch their back for the AI police.\n\nAnd so the words boiled in my stomach then shot out my fingers, and that’s that. I let them go.\n\nI’m sitting here reflecting on how this feature has felt crushing to many people, and yet, I’m feeling gratitude.\n\nBecause look at us. Look at you.\n\nLook how strong we are together. \n\nMy heart is feeling so full seeing so many people stand up in their own ways and say — \n\nNo. You can’t take away our humanity. Just like we don’t let AI take our humanity when we use it.\n\nWe all have experiences that are complicated, difficult to describe, beautiful, raw, and human. \n\nThat’s hard to hold as one person with a squishy brain that feels so many things all at once and doesn’t always have the words to express it. If AI helps you do that, then do it.\n\nThis is all to say, I’ve felt so much energy getting to interact with so many of you this week. I may not have slept as much as I should have 😅, but I am so grateful that you spent the time to stop by and say — Alyssa, I see you. \n\nI hope I’ve done the same for you.",
